Generated dictionary data — licence and attribution
===================================================

Files in this directory are generated by scripts/ingest/ from third-party
dictionary and corpus files. They are licensed separately from the application
code, which is MIT (see /LICENSE and /NOTICE). CEJC's derived aggregate has its
own terms, recorded in the CEJC carve-out below.


LICENCE
-------

Except for the CEJC and Tatoeba carve-outs below, files in this directory are
made available under the Creative Commons
Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International licence (CC BY-SA 4.0).

    Deed:      https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
    Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode

They are adaptations of CC BY-SA 4.0 dictionaries, so ShareAlike requires that
they carry the same licence.

The stroke-order files under strokes/ are adapted from KanjiVG, which is CC
BY-SA 3.0. That is an earlier version of the same ShareAlike licence; combining
it into this CC BY-SA 4.0 collection is the allowed upgrade direction, so they
carry CC BY-SA 4.0 here. See their entry under WHAT CAME FROM WHERE and
ATTRIBUTION.


WHAT CAME FROM WHERE
--------------------

  kanji.json               KANJIDIC2 (meanings, grade, stroke counts,
                           newspaper frequency) + KRADFILE (components)
  vocab.json               JMdict (written forms, readings, English glosses,
                           part-of-speech tags, frequency bands). The
                           `beginnerRank` field additionally derives from the
                           two JLPT lists (tanos.co.uk, open-anki-jlpt-decks)
                           and OpenSubtitles frequency (hermitdave/
                           FrequencyWords) — see their attribution below. At
                           runtime the CEJC teaching sequence in
                           cejc-reading-frequency.json replaces the curriculum
                           head; this stored rank remains the fallback order for
                           unscheduled Library rows.
  word-senses.json         JMdict (every reading a written form has, with the
                           glosses and part-of-speech tags of each). Same source
                           and same cut as vocab.json; it holds the forms JMdict
                           files under more than one reading, such as 人 as ひと,
                           じん and にん.
  word-definitions.json    JMdict's source sense boundaries and the readings
                           each sense permits, restored beside the older flat
                           word-senses snapshot without re-cutting word ranks.
  cejc-reading-frequency.json
                           CEJC Ver. 2022.09, reduced to aggregate lexical-
                           reading counts, lexical/POS teaching categories and
                           the approved core/essential teaching sequence. The
                           raw workbook is ignored and not redistributed.
  pitch.json               Kanjium pitch-accent database (the downstep position
                           of each word). See its attribution below.
  readings.json            KANJIDIC2 + JMdict (kanji readings aligned to the
                           words that anchor them)
  order.json               KANJIDIC2 + KRADFILE + JMdict (teaching order and
                           the everyday-word counts that drive it)
  components.json          KRADFILE + KANJIDIC2 (stroke counts of non-jouyou
                           components)
  confusable-derived.json  KRADFILE + KANJIDIC2 (kanji sharing a component
                           multiset and a stroke count)
  kanji-etymology.json     English Wiktionary (each kanji's glyph origin: the
                           FUNCTION of each component — semantic, phonetic or
                           form — the applicable SENSE, and the plain-language
                           glyph-origin explanation). See its attribution below.
  kanji-etymology-manual.json
                           Hand-authored extension of kanji-etymology.json. Same
                           schema; re-maps English Wiktionary's own glyph-origin
                           prose onto the visible modern glyph where the crawl's
                           by-glyph join could not (e.g. 時's phonetic, historically
                           之, now written 寺). Each entry cites its Wiktionary
                           source; CC BY-SA 4.0 like the crawled set.
  kanji-phonetic-gloss.json
                           Hand-authored glossary of the rare, untaught PHONETIC
                           components (冓, 莫, 韋 …) that carry an on-reading no
                           jouyou-only reading data can supply. On-readings and
                           short meanings are sourced per entry from English
                           Wiktionary and/or KANJIDIC2, both CC BY-SA 4.0.
  radicals.json            Unicode UCD (the 214 Kangxi radicals, their glyphs
                           and meanings) + KANJIDIC2 (radical stroke counts)
  radical-enrichment.json  Kanji Alive japanese-radicals.csv (each radical's
                           Japanese bushu name and positional variant forms) +
                           Unicode UCD EquivalentUnifiedIdeograph.txt (used only
                           to normalise variant glyphs to their CJK unified
                           form). See its attribution below. Kanji Alive is
                           CC BY 4.0 (no ShareAlike), so like Tatoeba it is
                           incorporated into this CC BY-SA 4.0 collection; the
                           combination is BY-SA, the permitted direction.
  kanji-radicals.json      KANJIDIC2 (each jouyou kanji's classical radical)
  grammar-corpus.json      Tatoeba (see carve-out)
  grammar-corpus-meta.json Tatoeba (build parameters and counts)
  grammar-corpus-dropped.json
                           Tatoeba (see carve-out) — the sentences the confound
                           audit removed from grammar-corpus.json, kept so the
                           removal can be reviewed rather than taken on trust.
                           Not shipped to the browser; nothing imports it.
  strokes/hiragana.json    KanjiVG (ordered stroke paths + stroke-number
  strokes/katakana.json    positions) — the 46 base hiragana, the 46 base
  strokes/kanji-NN.json    katakana, and the 2,136 jouyou kanji split across 48
  strokes/kanji-index.ts   chunks by codepoint. kanji-index.ts is generated
                           alongside them and carries the same data (the list of
                           characters ingested), so it is covered here too.


ATTRIBUTION
-----------

JMdict
  Everyday vocabulary: written forms, readings, English glosses, and
  part-of-speech tags.
  Copyright (c) Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group (EDRDG)
  Licence:  CC BY-SA 4.0
  Source:   https://www.edrdg.org/wiki/index.php/JMdict-EDICT_Dictionary_Project

Kanjium pitch-accent database
  The pitch.json downstep positions: for each word, the mora after which the
  voice falls (0 = heiban, no fall). Only rows the database records one way are
  kept; ambiguous ones are dropped rather than guessed.
  The pitch-accent notation and other additions to EDICT/KANJIDIC/KRADFILE were
  provided by Uros O. through his free database.
  Licence:  CC BY-SA 4.0
  Source:   https://github.com/mifunetoshiro/kanjium
            data/source_files/raw/accents.txt
  Being CC BY-SA 4.0, ShareAlike carries through to pitch.json, which is already
  under that licence.

KANJIDIC2
  Kanji meanings, on/kun readings, stroke counts, school grades, and newspaper
  frequency ranks.
  Copyright (c) Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group (EDRDG)
  Licence:  CC BY-SA 4.0
  Source:   https://www.edrdg.org/wiki/index.php/KANJIDIC_Project

KRADFILE
  The radical decomposition each kanji is built from.
  Copyright (c) Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group (EDRDG)
  Licence:  CC BY-SA 4.0
  Source:   https://www.edrdg.org/krad/kradinf.html

  The EDRDG licence, which covers all three files above and is the authority
  for their CC BY-SA 4.0 terms: https://www.edrdg.org/edrdg/licence.html

Unicode Character Database (UCD)
  The 214 Kangxi radicals in radicals.json: their number, canonical glyph, and
  meaning, read from the Kangxi Radicals block of UnicodeData.txt.
  Copyright (c) Unicode, Inc.
  Licence:  Unicode License (permissive; see terms)
  Source:   https://www.unicode.org/Public/UCD/latest/ucd/
  Terms:    https://www.unicode.org/license.txt

Kanji Alive (japanese-radicals.csv)
  Each classical Kangxi radical's Japanese bushu name (kana and romaji) and its
  positional variant forms, in radical-enrichment.json. The variant glyphs are
  Kanji Alive's radical-block codepoints normalised to their CJK unified
  ideograph via Unicode's EquivalentUnifiedIdeograph.txt (permissive Unicode
  License); the names and positions are Kanji Alive's own.
  Copyright (c) The Kanji Alive project
  Licence:  CC BY 4.0
  Source:   https://github.com/kanjialive/kanji-data-media
            language-data/japanese-radicals.csv
  Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode
  CC BY 4.0 carries no ShareAlike; incorporating it into this CC BY-SA 4.0
  collection is allowed (the collection is BY-SA). Attribution is required and
  is given here and in src/data/attribution.ts.

JLPT vocabulary lists (tanos.co.uk)
  One of two JLPT level lists whose consensus gates vocab.json's `beginnerRank`.
  Copyright (c) Jonathan Waller
  Licence:  CC BY
  Source:   http://www.tanos.co.uk/jlpt/
  A snapshot is committed at scripts/ingest/sources/jlpt-tanos.json.

open-anki-jlpt-decks
  The second, independent JLPT level list averaged into `beginnerRank`.
  Copyright (c) jamsinclair and contributors
  Licence:  MIT
  Source:   https://github.com/jamsinclair/open-anki-jlpt-decks
  Snapshots committed at scripts/ingest/sources/jlpt-anki-n[1-5].csv.

FrequencyWords — OpenSubtitles 2018 (Japanese)
  Secondary frequency ranks used in the fallback order for words CEJC does not
  observe.
  Copyright (c) Hermit Dave
  Licence:  CC BY-SA 4.0
  Source:   https://github.com/hermitdave/FrequencyWords
  A snapshot is committed at scripts/ingest/sources/opensubtitles-ja-2018.txt.
  Being CC BY-SA 4.0, ShareAlike carries through to vocab.json, which is already
  under that licence.

Corpus of Everyday Japanese Conversation (CEJC)
  Aggregate short-unit occurrence counts grouped by written lexeme and lexical
  reading. CEJC part of speech classifies core vocabulary, conversational
  essentials, grammar and fillers; occurrence counts order the word curriculum,
  order interchangeable readings within one dictionary definition, and identify
  a substantial everyday pronunciation preference.
  Copyright: National Institute for Japanese Language and Linguistics
  Terms:     Free for research and education; raw redistribution prohibited
  Version:   2022.09
  Source:    https://www2.ninjal.ac.jp/conversation/cejc/cejc-wc.html
  The ignored source archive is not included in this repository. See the CEJC
  carve-out below for cejc-reading-frequency.json.

KanjiVG
  Ordered, numbered stroke paths on a 109×109 grid, for hiragana, katakana and
  the jouyou kanji. Everything under strokes/ is generated from the
  per-character SVGs by scripts/ingest/kanjivg.mjs.
  Copyright (c) Ulrich Apel and the KanjiVG contributors
  Licence:  CC BY-SA 3.0
  Source:   https://github.com/KanjiVG/kanjivg  (https://kanjivg.tagaini.net)
  Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/legalcode
  CC BY-SA 3.0 → 4.0 is an allowed upgrade, so this data ships CC BY-SA 4.0 with
  the rest of the directory; the in-app "how it's written" section also credits
  KanjiVG where the strokes are shown.

Tatoeba
  Example sentences and their human English translations.
  Copyright (c) The Tatoeba Project and its contributors
  Licence:  CC BY 2.0 FR
  Source:   https://tatoeba.org/eng/downloads
  Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/fr/legalcode

English Wiktionary
  Each jouyou kanji's glyph origin, parsed from the Translingual
  {{Han compound}} markup and the Glyph-origin prose: the FUNCTION each
  component plays (semantic / phonetic / form), the applicable SENSE gloss, and
  a mechanically cleaned plain-language explanation. Generated by
  scripts/ingest/kanji-etymology.mjs.
  Copyright (c) Wiktionary contributors
  Licence:  CC BY-SA 4.0
  Source:   https://en.wiktionary.org/
  Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode
  Being CC BY-SA 4.0, ShareAlike carries through to kanji-etymology.json, which
  is already under that licence.


CEJC CARVE-OUT
--------------

cejc-reading-frequency.json is a mechanically reduced educational analysis of
CEJC Ver. 2022.09. It contains no audio, video, transcript, source row, speaker
metadata or raw workbook. It is not offered under CC BY-SA and this repository
does not purport to relicense NINJAL's work. Use of that file remains subject to
CEJC's terms. The raw archive lives only under the gitignored
scripts/ingest/raw/ directory.


TATOEBA CARVE-OUT
-----------------

grammar-corpus.json, grammar-corpus-meta.json and grammar-corpus-dropped.json
derive from Tatoeba only. No EDRDG material is in them.

Tatoeba is CC BY 2.0 FR: attribution, no ShareAlike. Nothing here can add a
ShareAlike condition to Tatoeba's sentences, and this licence does not try to.
If you want the sentences and translations, you may take them from these files
under CC BY 2.0 FR, attributing the Tatoeba Project and its contributors.

Every sentence keeps its Tatoeba id in the `id` field, so any sentence can be
traced to its upstream entry and its author.

The selection, filtering and pattern annotations layered over those sentences
(the `n`, `v`, `p` and `sp` fields) are this project's own work, offered under
CC BY-SA 4.0 for consistency with the rest of this directory.


NOT A SOURCE
------------

The build pipeline uses fugashi and UniDic (unidic-lite) to segment and
part-of-speech-tag Japanese text. Neither is redistributed here, and the tokens
recorded in grammar-corpus.json are the surface forms of the Tatoeba sentences
themselves. UniDic is therefore a build-time tool, not a source of this data,
and is not attributed as one.
